#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 840.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120601} :activity/id #uuid "291c939d-6c10-57d8-bddb-ee6fe28fa722" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048151 :topic/id #uuid "838443f8-2681-52c2-95ba-8d93abeaf009" :topic/math-academy-id 1839} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120601 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121011 :tutorial/content "For a plane curve $C$ defined by the vector-valued function\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t) = \\langle x(t), \\: y(t) \\rangle,\n$$\n\nthe *curvature* of $C$ at an arbitrary point can be found using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\kappa(t)  = \\dfrac{|x'(t)y''(t) - y'(t)x''(t)|}{ (x'(t)^2 + y'(t)^2)^{3/2}}\n$$\n\nAdditionally, the **signed curvature** $k$ is given by\n\n$$\nk(t) = \\dfrac{x'(t)y''(t) - y'(t)x''(t)}{ (x'(t)^2 + y'(t)^2)^{3/2}}.\n$$\n\nFor example, consider a curve defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx(t) = 2t, \\qquad\ny(t) = 1+t^2.\n$$\n\nLet's find the curvature of this curve at the point where $t=1.$\n\nFirst, we calculate the first and second derivatives of the components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {x}^{′}(t)=2,\\, & {y}^{′}(t)=2t \\\\  & {x}^{″}(t)=0,\\, & {y}^{″}(t)=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these expressions into our curvature formula, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(t) & =\\frac{|2\\cdot 2-2t\\cdot 0|}{{[{2}^{2}+(2t)^{2}]}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|\\,4\\,|}{{2}^{3}{[1+{t}^{2}]}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2{({t}^{2}+1)}^{3/2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature at $t =1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(1) & =\\frac{1}{2{({1}^{2}+1)}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "b40dd6c7-fe0a-5126-9014-ec7c0d5eb29b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2534 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "402c2124-8063-56c7-bb37-ea2cdcc0a66c" :step/math-academy-id 11439 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120606}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120602 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119761 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119577 :question/id #uuid "1f374865-c7f7-510c-b361-fbad352d86c6" :question/math-academy-id "e-3452"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "36986a18-172c-5983-8f6f-3211b6e8f89d" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048339}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120370} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120499} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281465} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281468} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281469} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281470} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281473} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281474} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281477} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444929}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Radius of Curvature of a Plane Curve Given in Parametric Form"} :step/id #uuid "71cffa1e-47f4-55d9-a319-71cf51f73286" :step/math-academy-id 11440 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120604}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120603 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121012 :tutorial/content "We've been using the following formula for unsigned curvature:\n\n$$\n\\kappa (t)  = \\dfrac{|x'(t)y''(t) - y'(t)x''(t)|}{ (x'(t)^2 + y'(t)^2)^{3/2}}\n$$\n\nTo see where this formula comes from, remember that for a curve $\\mathbf r(t) = \\langle x(t),  y(t),  z(t)\\rangle,$ the unsigned curvature formula is\n\n$$\n\\kappa(t) = \\dfrac{\\|\\mathbf r'(t) \\times \\mathbf r''(t)\\|}{\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|^3}.\n$$\n\nIf a curve does not have a $z$-component, then we may consider $z(t) = 0.$ Thus, any plane curve $\\mathbf r(t) = \\langle x(t), \\: y(t) \\rangle$ can be represented as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t) = \\langle x(t), \\:  y(t), \\: 0\\rangle.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate $\\mathbf r'(t)$ and $\\mathbf r''(t)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r'(t) = \\langle x'(t), y'(t),0 \\rangle, \\qquad \\mathbf r''(t) = \\langle x''(t), y''(y),0 \\rangle\n$$\n\nSubstituting these expressions into our cross-product formula, we find that the curvature $\\kappa(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(t) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{‖⟨{x}^{′}(t),{y}^{′}(t),0⟩\\times ⟨{x}^{″}(t),{y}^{″}(t),0⟩‖}{‖⟨{x}^{′}(t),{y}^{′}(t)⟩{‖}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{‖⟨0,0,{x}^{′}(t){y}^{″}(t)-{y}^{′}(t){x}^{″}(t)⟩‖}{(\\sqrt{{x}^{′}(t)^{2}+{y}^{′}(t)^{2}})^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|{x}^{′}(t){y}^{″}(t)-{y}^{′}(t){x}^{″}(t)|}{({x}^{′}(t)^{2}+{y}^{′}(t)^{2})^{3/2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "16d4e0f1-81e9-5252-9390-52f2e8512d48" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2535 :tutorial/title "Deriving the Formula for the Curvature of a Plane Parametric Curve"} :step/id #uuid "f6ca150c-baf4-5085-bb7d-62a05a15bc30" :step/math-academy-id 11441} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120604 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121013 :tutorial/content "Suppose we have a plane curve defined by the explicit equation \n\n$$\ny = y(x).\n$$\n\nIn this case, we can parametrize this curve as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t) = \\langle t, \\: y(t) \\rangle.\n$$\n\nNow, using the fact that $x=t,$ we calculate the first and second derivatives of the components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & =1,\\, & {y}^{′}(t) & ={y}^{′}(x) \\\\ {x}^{″}(t) & =0,\\, & {y}^{″}(t) & ={y}^{″}(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the formula for the (unsigned) curvature, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(x) & =\\frac{|{x}^{′}(t){y}^{″}(t)-{y}^{′}(t){x}^{″}(t)|}{([{x}^{′}(t)]^{2}+[{y}^{′}(t)]^{2})^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|1\\cdot {y}^{″}(x)-{y}^{′}(x)\\cdot 0|}{({1}^{2}+[{y}^{′}(x)]^{2})^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|{y}^{″}(x)|}{(1+[{y}^{′}(x)]^{2})^{3/2}}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the unsigned curvature for $y=y(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\kappa (x) = \\dfrac{|y''(x)|}{ (1 + [y'(x)]^2)^{3/2}}\n$$\n\nand the signed curvature is\n\n$$\nk(x) = \\dfrac {y''(x)} {(1+[y'(x)]^2)^{3/2}}\t.\n$$\n\nNote that the signed curvature $k(x)$ has the same sign as $y''(x).$ Therefore,\n\n- if $k(x) > 0,$ the curve is concave up, and\n\n- if $k(x) < 0,$ the curve is concave down." :tutorial/id #uuid "66d1756a-304e-51f3-9622-3074a6e63c70" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2536 :tutorial/title "The Curvature of a Plane Curve Given in Cartesian Form"} :step/id #uuid "e2fbbaa6-20ed-599e-ba35-826ad87a33df" :step/math-academy-id 11444 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120605}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120605 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119762 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119578 :question/id #uuid "2ff129e7-a923-5510-a35b-a6be403152ac" :question/math-academy-id "e-3453"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "384072a8-f008-5279-bb58-8086eb42b710" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120347} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120377} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235463} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281485} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281486} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281488} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281489} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281490} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444928}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Curvature of a Plane Curve Given in Cartesian Form"} :step/id #uuid "a177d231-4807-586b-8e78-fdd08a9a7171" :step/math-academy-id 11445 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120603}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120606 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119763 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119579 :question/id #uuid "d8e470ad-6a9a-5a03-a90e-b21b24cb6862" :question/math-academy-id "e-3454"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "814655d8-f459-5df5-b722-3024b87065db" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048648}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120345} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120378} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281460} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281461} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281472} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401343} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401350} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444930} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444931} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444932}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Curvature of a Plane Curve Given in Parametric Form"} :step/id #uuid "8c7bc743-0c19-5cb7-bd3d-35d253cf54f2" :step/math-academy-id 1144